<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xi Jinping</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">b. <a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xi Jinping is a Chinese politician who has been general secretary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> since 2012 and president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> since 2013. The son of the revolutionary leader Xi Zhongxun, he has concentrated power to a degree not seen since Mao, led a sweeping anti-corruption campaign and removed the presidential term limit in 2018. He began a third term as party leader in 2022.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xi-jinping.jpg" alt="Xi Jinping" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Simon Dawson / No 10 Downing Street · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prime_Minister_Keir_Starmer_visits_China_(55066713683)_(cropped%2Bangle).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Xi Jinping</b>, general secretary since 2012 and president since 2013, has recentralised authority, led a sweeping anti-corruption campaign, removed presidential term limits in 2018 and began a third term as party leader in 2022.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Xi Jinping</b> the state has taken a more favourable view, letting temples register as folk belief activity sites.</blockquote><a href="/religion/chinese-folk-religion">Chinese folk religion · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In China the ancient schools of Confucianism and Legalism have returned to mainstream thought, and <b>Xi Jinping</b> has called traditional Chinese culture the “soul” of the nation.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/conservatism">Conservatism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholars and commentators have cited Legalism as an influence on the governance of the People’s Republic of China, particularly under <b>Xi Jinping</b>; Sam Crane has called the modern Chinese state “Confucianism on the outside, Legalist on the inside”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/legalism">Legalism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Xi Jinping</b>, in a speech of 2013, summed it up as seeking truth from facts, the mass line, and national independence and autonomy.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/maoism">Maoism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Xi Jinping</b> sang it with the performers at the galas for the 15th anniversary of Macau's handover (2014) and the 20th of Hong Kong's (2017), and it accompanied China's entry at the opening ceremonies of the 2022 Winter Olympics and Paralympics and the 2022 Asian Games, held in…</blockquote><a href="/songs/ode-to-the-motherland">Ode to the Motherland</a></li></ul></section>
